{
  "id": "COMPOUND:27871 - PROTEIN:6326",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which 11Alpha-Mangostanin may interact with Phosphodiesterase 6C, through a series of molecular targets that ultimately converge on this enzyme. The drug is implicated in the inhibition of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, a key component of the polycomb repressive complex 2 (PRC2), which is involved in chromatin modification and gene regulation. This complex is targeted by multiple compounds, including 2,4,6-Trihydroxy-3-(3,3-Dimethylallyl)Acetophenone and (1R,2S,4aS,8aS)-1-[(E)-5-hydroxy-3-methylpent-3-enyl]-2,5,5,8a-tetramethyl-3,4,4a,6,7,8-hexahydro-1H-naphthalen-2-ol, which in turn inhibit Phosphodiesterase 6C. The shared pathway indicates that 11Alpha-Mangostanin may exert its effects through a similar mechanism, possibly by modulating the PRC2 complex and thereby influencing the activity of Phosphodiesterase 6C. The consistent scores across the three paths suggest a robust and well-supported relationship, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
